<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Achieng wants the single matrix for the rotation of <m>90^\circ</m> anticlockwise about O followed by the reflection in the line <m>y = -x</m>, and the final image of <m>K({3},\,{-3})</m>. Her working has exactly one mistake.</p><ol><li>Matrix of the first transformation: <m>A = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} 0 &amp; -1 \\ 1 &amp; 0 \end{array}\right]}</m>.</li><li>Matrix of the second transformation: <m>B = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} 0 &amp; -1 \\ -1 &amp; 0 \end{array}\right]}</m>.</li><li>The single matrix is <m>A \times B</m>.</li><li><m>A \times B</m> <m>= {\left[\begin{array}{cc} 1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; -1 \end{array}\right]}</m>.</li><li>Final image of K: <m>({3},\,{3})</m>.</li></ol><p>(a) Which step has the mistake?</p><div>[ Step 1 | Step 2 | Step 3 | Step 4 | Step 5 ]  </div><p>(b) Give the correct single matrix.</p><div><fillin characters="3" name="ans2"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) The mistake is in Step 3. The first transformation's matrix goes on the right, so the single matrix is <m>B \times A</m>, not <m>A \times B</m>.</p><p>(b) <m>BA = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} 0 &amp; -1 \\ -1 &amp; 0 \end{array}\right]}{\left[\begin{array}{cc} 0 &amp; -1 \\ 1 &amp; 0 \end{array}\right]} = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} -1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 \end{array}\right]}</m>, and the final image of K is <m>({-3},\,{-3})</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p>Step 3</p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\left[\begin{array}{cc} -1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 \end{array}\right]</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>